  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to combine Fourier analysis with a novel optimization strategy to construct a piecewise constant function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. Unlike previous approaches that used random initialization and gradient-based optimization on a fixed grid, our method leverages the structure of optimal solutions in terms of amplitude modulation and harmonic content. We represent the function using a sparse Fourier basis (selected via optimization) and optimize their amplitudes and phases to minimize the maximum overlap. This approach balances global exploration and local refinement, and can adaptively adjust the resolution. The rationale is that functions with low-frequency components are more likely to have lower maximum overlap, and our optimization ensures that the solution satisfies the integral constraint exactly while maintaining non-negativity.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nWe parametrize the function on n points using a sum of Fourier modes:\nh(x) = 0.5 + sum_{k=1}^{K} (A_k * cos(2\u03c0*k*x/L) + B_k * sin(2\u03c0*k*x/L))\nwhere L=2 is our domain length. The function has mean 0.5, and the Fourier parts integrate to zero, satisfying the integral constraint \u222bh(x)dx=1 automatically.\n\nThe optimization objective is to minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx. We use a smooth approximation (fractional log-sum-exp) of the max operation combined with dx normalization, with on the grid x_i = (i-0.5)/n * L.\n\nConstraints are enforced by projection: after optimization, we clip values to [0,1] and adjust to satisfy the integral constraint by replacing the mean if necessary.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Parent Validation:\n   - If `initial_h_values` is provided, validate that it has exactly `parent_n` points.\n   - For other dimensions, use interpolation and projection.\n\n2. Initialization:\n   - Use intersected Fourier projections of the parent solution.\n   - Generate random Fourier bases with controlled frequency content.\n   - Include smooth modulation initializations.\n\n3. Core Optimization:\n   - Use a Bayesian optimization strategy with model-based search.\n   - Maintain a diverse population of candidate solutions.\n   - Use tree-based optimization with parallel evaluations.\n   - Periodically update the objective model and select promising candidates.\n\n4. Candidate Acceptance:\n   - Track the global minimum so far.\n   - Monitor convergence by tracking improvements over time.\n   - Implement restarts for complex cases.\n\n5. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Optimize over K (number of modes) from 1 to 10.\n   - Use frequency bandwidths that are powers of 2.\n   - Control the total bandwidth used.\n\n6. Budget and Robustness:\n   - Split the budget for exploration vs. exploitation.\n   - Use fallback strategies when optimization stalls.\n   - Implement dimension transfer using interpolation.\n   - Use dimension switching based on time budget.\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n1. Ensure feasibility: 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)=0.5*n.\n2. Re-evaluate using actual task metric.\n3. Maintain detailed logs of the optimization process.",
